        "start": "11-01",
        "end": "03-31",
        "note": "Brumation, the reptile equivalent of hibernation, spent in communal hibernacula such as deep burrows and rock piles; snakes emerge when temperatures rise in spring."
      },
      {
        "name": "mating",
        "start": "04-01",
        "end": "05-31",
        "note": "Mating takes place in spring after brumation, when temperatures rise; females may store sperm over winter."
      },
      {
        "name": "young",
        "start": "08-01",
        "end": "09-30",
        "note": "Ovoviviparous: broods of eight to twelve young are born in August and September."
      },
      {
        "name": "territorial",
        "start": "03-15",
        "end": "05-15",
        "note": "Spring activity on the surface: the species basks to reach operating temperature before foraging."
      },
      {
        "name": "breeding",
        "start": "05-01",
        "end": "09-30",
        "region": "US-CO",
        "note": "'The western terrestrial garter snake does not lay eggs, but instead is ovoviviparous, which is characteristic of natricine snakes. Broods of eight to 12 young are born in August and September.' Because the young are born rather than laid, this phase covers the whole active season rather than a courtship window."
      }
